the film's first stop was the Indonesian  island of Borneo home of one of the  world's largest wild orangutan  populations Borneo is very very remote  and we had to take 30,000 pounds of  production equipment upriver on small  boats into the jungle the IMAX crew made  this journey because the world renowned  primatologist dr. Berube mary galdikas  offered them rare access to her work of  studying and saving orangutans 40 years  ago  there were no roads no telephones that  was rich like no electricity you see  what it is that she had to deal with you  know it was swamps the leeches the  insects the heat the humidity I mean  this is one of the most uncompromising  places that I've ever been and she came  here back in the early 70s and built 80  kilometers of trail system to get you  know her deepest jungles of Borneo and  pioneered from nothing this whole study  of orangutan back then they weren't that  habituated so they peed loose spots up  into trees and to walk through everyday  through the rainforest how dense it is  to study these spots up into trees  was not only daunting monumental I think  what attracted me to other towns was  their eyes they have a certain kind of  calmer sweet serenity you come here and  here you are in this amazing forest  that's been preserved and it's  exhilarating because you step off the  boat onto the dock and immediately you  start seeing orangutangs you know coming  over forests almost to say hello  it's magical right here in this paradise  orangutans are under enormous pressure  from palm oil plantations huge tracts of  tropical rainforests specially Warnie  are being leveled to make way for palm  oil plantation obliterating orangutan  habitat and deranged our population is  just getting smaller and smaller every  day I really love around the towns I  think we have a very strong  responsibility to make sure they don't  go extinct and the only way we can do  that is by saving the horse
 

